# XSH perror()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.perror.001
anchor     functions/perror.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With errno ENOENT, perror("pfx") writes exactly "pfx: ", the
           string strerror(ENOENT) returns, and a newline to the standard
           error stream.
test       t/001-prefix-message.c

assertion  XSH.perror.002
anchor     functions/perror.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With errno EACCES, perror(NULL) writes exactly the string
           strerror(EACCES) returns and a newline, with no prefix, colon or
           space.
test       t/002-null-string.c

assertion  XSH.perror.003
anchor     functions/perror.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With errno EINVAL, perror("") writes exactly the string
           strerror(EINVAL) returns and a newline, with no colon or space.
test       t/003-empty-string.c

assertion  XSH.perror.004
anchor     functions/perror.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       For errno EPERM, ENOENT, EINTR, EIO, EACCES, EEXIST, EINVAL,
           ERANGE, 0 and 100000 in turn, perror("p") writes "p: ", the string
           strerror() returns for that value, and a newline, so that the file
           holds the ten lines in order.
note       The message for 0 and for 100000 is whatever strerror() gives (XSH
           strerror(): any int maps to a message, and for zero the message is
           empty or says no error); the test compares, it does not name the
           text.
test       t/004-same-as-strerror.c

assertion  XSH.perror.005
anchor     functions/perror.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5
class      required
since      1988
says       On a stderr that has no orientation yet, fwide(stderr, 0) is 0
           after perror("p") has written its message.
note       XSH 2.5: with no orientation, perror() behaves as a byte output
           function except that it does not set the orientation. The test is
           UNRESOLVED if stderr is already oriented when it starts.
test       t/005-orientation.c

assertion  XSH.perror.006
anchor     functions/perror.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5
class      required
since      1988
says       With stderr made byte-oriented by fputc('A', stderr), perror("p")
           with errno ENOENT then appends to the same stream, so that the
           file holds "Ap: ", the string strerror(ENOENT) returns, and a
           newline.
note       XSH 2.5: on a stream that is already byte-oriented, perror()
           behaves as the byte output functions, which write as if by
           successive fputc() calls, so the bytes keep their order on the one
           stream.
test       t/006-byte-oriented.c

assertion  XSH.perror.007
anchor     functions/perror.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/perror.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      1988
says       With descriptor 2 open for reading only and stderr unbuffered,
           perror("p") called with errno ENOENT leaves the error indicator of
           stderr set and errno EBADF.
note       The error is that of fputc() (the page refers to it for ERRORS):
           EBADF for a descriptor not open for writing, which applies whether
           the stream is unbuffered or its buffer needs flushing. Where
           setvbuf() refuses _IONBF (XSH 2.5) and the message stays in a
           buffer, the result is UNRESOLVED.
test       t/007-error-indicator.c

assertion  XSH.perror.008
anchor     functions/perror.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With descriptor 2 on a regular file whose modification time is set
           to 2001-09-09, after perror("p") and a successful fflush(stderr),
           fstat() shows a later st_mtim.
note       XBD 4.12: marked timestamps are updated before fstat() returns.
           The change time is not checked: telling it apart from the
           futimens() of the setup needs the clock to move past the file
           system's granularity.
test       t/008-mtime.c
